// Runtime-only marker added by OrbSpawnUtility.SpawnWithPop (never authored on a prototype) - a
// dropped currency/scrap orb pops out on a short ballistic arc from the enemy's death position
// instead of teleporting straight to a scattered landing spot at the enemy's own death Y.
// PopMotionSystem integrates gravity each tick and removes this the instant the arc lands, same
// "settled = free to iterate afterward" contract GroundOffset.Enabled/GroundSettleSystem already
// use - landing also clears GroundOffset.Enabled, since the orb is placed exactly at its resting
// height and has nothing left to settle toward.
component PopVelocity
{
    FPVector3 Velocity;

    // Real ground height under the position this orb popped FROM (the enemy's death point), resolved
    // once by OrbSpawnUtility.SpawnWithPop. PopMotionSystem refuses to let the arc carry the orb up
    // onto anything meaningfully higher than this, so a drop can never come to rest on a raised
    // platform the player has no reason to climb for a coin - it bumps the platform's edge and falls
    // back down onto the floor its enemy actually died on.
    //
    // Only CLIMBING is blocked, never falling: an enemy killed on a ledge still drops coins that can
    // scatter off it and land below, which reads fine and is often the only reachable outcome anyway.
    FP OriginGroundY;

    // Opt-out of the climb block above. False (every currency/scrap drop) keeps the original
    // behaviour: a coin may never come to rest meaningfully higher than the floor it dropped from,
    // because a coin the player has to go and climb for is a chore, not a decision.
    //
    // True for a dropped Signature Accessory (see docs/accessory-guard.md), which is the exact
    // opposite case: it is SUPPOSED to be able to land somewhere awkward, including a platform above
    // you. Going and getting it back is the mechanic, not a tax on a reward you already earned.
    Boolean CanLandHigher;
}
